export const meta = {
  name: 'design-rank-one-producer-route',
  description: 'Judge panel: design the Lean route to the rank-one canonical divisor classifier, AbelInverse, and IsOpen',
  phases: [{ title: 'Design' }, { title: 'Judge' }],
}

const CTX = '/tmp/ajcr-design-context.md'
const AJCR = '/home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon/MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge-Rebuild'

const PLAN = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    plan: { type: 'string', description: 'The full design in markdown: theorem statements (Lean-shaped), proof strategies mapped to existing lemmas, file placement, dependency order, session-sized first unit' },
    risks: { type: 'string', description: 'Hidden blockers, unverified assumptions, and what could sink this route' },
  },
  required: ['plan', 'risks'],
}

const COMMON = `Read ${CTX} first — it is the ground truth for what exists and what is missing. You are designing Lean 4 proof architecture for the AJCR project at ${AJCR}. You may READ any file in the repo to verify signatures (use grep + Read; the key files are named in the context doc). Do NOT edit anything, do NOT run lake build (a build is already running; single-compiler policy). Verify every existing-lemma claim you rely on by actually reading its statement in the source. Your output must be Lean-shaped: concrete theorem statements with the real types from this codebase (DivFamZarAff C A (genus C), PicRankOneOpen pi, Over.sigmaExtension, etc.), not hand-waving.`

phase('Design')
const angles = [
  {
    label: 'uniqueness-first',
    model: 'fable',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

ANGLE: uniqueness-first. The keystone claim: for a class lam in PicRankOneOpen over affine base A, any two DivFamZarAff C A (genus C) with Abel class lam are DivEq-equal. Design this theorem precisely:
(1) The exact Lean statement (what "same Abel class" should mean here: abelDivAffPlus? relPicMk of picClass? — check which equality is strongest/available from the glue theorem's conjuncts).
(2) A complete proof strategy mapped to existing lemmas: presumably via stalk ideals (divEq_of_stalkIdeal_eq), the canonical section machinery (DivisorDatumInverse), rank-one H0 uniqueness up to unit (existsUnique_unit_* in Pic0RankOnePresentation), and Zariski-locality (divEq_of_divEq_pullback). Where exactly does rank-one-ness enter, and in what form is it available over A (note: the native presentation lives over an étale cover of A, not A itself — does uniqueness need the presentation over A, or can DivEq be checked after faithfully-flat pullback to the cover? Is there a faithfully-flat/étale analogue of divEq_of_divEq_pullback, and if not, what would proving DivEq-descends-along-Spec-of-faithfully-flat need?).
(3) How uniqueness then yields: presentation-independence of the glued divisor, the cocycle condition for descent, naturality, and AbelInverse — each as a named corollary with its Lean statement.
(4) The first session-sized kernel-clean unit.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'descent-first',
    model: 'fable',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

ANGLE: descent-first. Assume rank-one divisor uniqueness over any affine base is available as a black box (state the interface you need from it). Design the passage from the finite-glue output F0 : DivFamZarAff C P.cover.Carrier (genus C) to the global classifier:
(1) Descend F0 to the base algebra A. Candidate routes to evaluate against the actual code: (a) via representability — F0 corresponds to a map Spec(cover.Carrier) -> (divRepAffGenusScheme C).left via divFunctorAff_genus_representableBy; the cocycle over cover.Carrier ⊗[A] cover.Carrier comes from uniqueness; then descend the MORPHISM along the étale/faithfully-flat affine cover (what does Descent/ offer for descending morphisms into a (non-affine) scheme? Read Descent/AffineSchemeDescent.lean, AmitsurEqualizer.lean, and check whether divRepAffGenusScheme is affine or has any useful cover structure); (b) direct effective descent of the divisor family data (LocalEquations along relCurve base change); (c) any smarter route the code suggests (e.g. is there an fppf/étale sheaf property already proved for divFunctorAff or PicEtAff — grep PicEtSheaf, DescentClass, descentClasses!). Recommend ONE with justification.
(2) From the affine-natural canonical divisor to the big-site natural transformation divisor : rankOneLocus ⟶ rankOneDivisorLocus (Over.sigmaExtension values are over arbitrary schemes S — how does the affine construction extend? Check how rankOneLocus.obj (op S) unfolds: it is a sigma of a structure map S -> Spec k and a locus element over Over.mk of it; membership only quantifies over AFFINE test maps t : overSpec k A ⟶ T.unop. So constructing divisor.app at a non-affine S needs Zariski gluing over an affine open cover of S with uniqueness for cocycles — design this, checking what gluing tools exist for maps into the divisor scheme / divRankOnePresentationPreimageRepresenter subfunctor).
(3) The per-(X,g) PicRankOneFibrePresentationInput: how to construct W (the locus where the displayed family is rank-one — presumably the preimage open along g of ... what? this is subtle since PicRankOneOpen is not yet known open! Check whether W can be taken as the union of affine opens where membership holds, and what exists_factor then needs) and the EvaluationDivisorPullback square.
(4) The first session-sized kernel-clean unit on this route.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'minimal-committable',
    model: 'opus',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

ANGLE: risk-first / minimal-committable. You are the skeptic. (1) Enumerate the hidden blockers in the route (uniqueness -> descent -> classifier -> AbelInverse -> IsOpen) that the optimistic designs will miss: e.g. DivEq descent along faithfully flat (not open immersion) covers; the quotient DivFamZarAff having no canonical representative (choice issues in defining a NATURAL transformation from a quotient); universe issues in Over.sigmaExtension; the W-open in FibrePresented needing pointwise openness that is exactly what we are trying to prove (circularity risk!); Noetherian hypotheses (IsNoetherianRing P.cover.Carrier) vs arbitrary A; whether rankOneDivisorLocus membership (divRankOnePresentationPreimageRepresenter) can even be verified for the descended divisor. Read the actual definitions to confirm or dismiss each.
(2) Determine what the MAXIMAL genuinely-landable kernel-clean unit is for ONE session (~2-3 focused hours of proving) that strictly reduces the family-level gap per the stop rule (immediate downstream consumer must compile). Candidates to evaluate: (a) the affine uniqueness theorem alone with a consumer (presentation-independence of the glued divisor); (b) DivEq faithfully-flat descent lemma; (c) the AbelInverse from uniqueness given classifier — pick what is real.
(3) Sanity-check the whole endgame: does anything in the chain pic0_sepClosed_representableBy -> finite-Galois -> pic0_representableBy have a SHORTER honest path given what is in Descent/ and the AJC project? Answer with evidence from files.`,
  },
]

const designs = await parallel(angles.map(a => () =>
  agent(a.prompt, { label: a.label, phase: 'Design', schema: PLAN, model: a.model, effort: 'high' })
))

phase('Judge')
const valid = designs.filter(Boolean)
const judgePrompt = `${COMMON}

You are the JUDGE synthesizing three independent designs for the same task. Here they are:

${valid.map((d, i) => `=== DESIGN ${i + 1} (${angles[i].label}) ===\nPLAN:\n${d.plan}\n\nRISKS:\n${d.risks}`).join('\n\n')}

Produce the FINAL implementation plan for this session and the next ones:
(1) Adjudicate disagreements between designs by checking the code where needed.
(2) A concrete ordered theorem list: for each — file (new or existing), Lean-shaped statement, proof strategy in 2-5 sentences naming the exact existing lemmas, estimated difficulty (S/M/L/XL).
(3) THE FIRST UNIT to implement right now (session-sized, kernel-clean, with an immediate consumer that compiles). Be decisive.
(4) What to explicitly NOT attempt this session and why.`

const final = await agent(judgePrompt, { label: 'judge-synthesis', phase: 'Judge', schema: PLAN, model: 'fable', effort: 'high' })
return { final, designs: Object.fromEntries(angles.map((a, i) => [a.label, designs[i] || 'FAILED'])) }
